## 3) `background.js`

This sets up default storage and optionally lets you toggle blocking in the future.

```js
const DEFAULT_SETTINGS = {
  enabled: true
};

chrome.runtime.onInstalled.addListener(async () => {
  const current = await chrome.storage.local.get(DEFAULT_SETTINGS);
  if (typeof current.enabled !== "boolean") {
    await chrome.storage.local.set(DEFAULT_SETTINGS);
  }
});

chrome.runtime.onMessage.addListener((message, sender, sendResponse) => {
  if (message?.type === "GET_STATUS") {
    chrome.storage.local.get(DEFAULT_SETTINGS).then((data) => {
      sendResponse({ enabled: data.enabled });
    });
    return true;
  }

  if (message?.type === "SET_ENABLED") {
    const enabled = !!message.enabled;
    chrome.storage.local.set({ enabled }).then(() => {
      sendResponse({ ok: true, enabled });
    });
    return true;
  }
});
```

Note: With static `declarativeNetRequest` rules, you typically can’t truly “disable” them by just setting a flag unless you also manage dynamic rules or enable/disable rule resources. This starter implementation stores the setting and can be extended to switch between rule sets.

