Below is a **minimal, streaming, low-memory parser** example in **Rust**. It reads from standard input in chunks, parses line-by-line incrementally, and never loads the full input into memory.

This version assumes an input format of **one record per line**, where each line is:

```text
timestamp,value
```

It parses each line, counts records, sums values, and avoids allocating per line except for a small reusable buffer.

## Rust example

```rust
use std::io::{self, Read};

fn main() -> io::Result<()> {
    let stdin = io::stdin();
    let mut handle = stdin.lock();

    // 64 KiB buffer
    let mut buf = [0u8; 64 * 1024];
    let mut carry = Vec::new();

    let mut total_records: u64 = 0;
    let mut total_value: i64 = 0;

    loop {
        let n = handle.read(&mut buf)?;
        if n == 0 {
            break;
        }

        carry.extend_from_slice(&buf[..n]);

        // Process complete lines in `carry`
        let mut start = 0;
        for i in 0..carry.len() {
            if carry[i] == b'\n' {
                let line = &carry[start..i];
                if !line.is_empty() {
                    if let Some((ts, val)) = parse_line(line) {
                        // Example processing
                        total_records += 1;
                        total_value += val;
                        let _ = ts; // suppress unused warning
                    }
                }
                start = i + 1;
            }
        }

        // Keep only the incomplete tail
        if start > 0 {
            carry.drain(..start);
        }
    }

    // Handle final line if no trailing newline
    if !carry.is_empty() {
        if let Some((ts, val)) = parse_line(&carry) {
            total_records += 1;
            total_value += val;
            let _ = ts;
        }
    }

    eprintln!("records={}, total_value={}", total_records, total_value);
    Ok(())
}

fn parse_line(line: &[u8]) -> Option<(u64, i64)> {
    let comma = line.iter().position(|&b| b == b',')?;
    let ts = std::str::from_utf8(&line[..comma]).ok()?.parse::<u64>().ok()?;
    let val = std::str::from_utf8(&line[comma + 1..]).ok()?.parse::<i64>().ok()?;
    Some((ts, val))
}
```

## Why this is memory-efficient

- Reads input in fixed-size chunks
- Only retains the incomplete tail between reads
- Processes each record as soon as a line is complete
- Avoids loading gigabyte-scale input into memory

