Below is a **minimal working example** of a real-time rigid-body-style physics simulation with **frame-rate constraints**, written in **<language>C++</language>**. It simulates a ball under gravity bouncing on the ground using a **fixed timestep** loop, which is the standard approach for stable real-time physics.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <chrono>
#include <thread>
#include <vector>
#include <cmath>

struct Body {
    double y;      // position
    double vy;     // velocity
    double radius; // shape size
    double mass;
};

int main() {
    const double gravity = -9.81;
    const double restitution = 0.8;   // bounce energy retention
    const double fixed_dt = 1.0 / 60.0; // 60 Hz physics update
    const int target_fps = 60;
    const auto frame_time = std::chrono::milliseconds(1000 / target_fps);

    Body ball{10.0, 0.0, 0.5, 1.0};

    auto previous = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
    double accumulator = 0.0;

    while (true) {
        auto current = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
        std::chrono::duration<double> elapsed = current - previous;
        previous = current;

        accumulator += elapsed.count();

        // Physics updates at fixed timestep
        while (accumulator >= fixed_dt) {
            // Integrate motion
            ball.vy += gravity * fixed_dt;
            ball.y += ball.vy * fixed_dt;

            // Ground collision at y = radius
            if (ball.y < ball.radius) {
                ball.y = ball.radius;
                ball.vy = -ball.vy * restitution;

                // Prevent tiny jitter
                if (std::abs(ball.vy) < 0.1) {
                    ball.vy = 0.0;
                }
            }

            accumulator -= fixed_dt;
        }

        // Simple text output
        std::cout << "\rBall height: " << ball.y
                  << "   velocity: " << ball.vy << "      " << std::flush;

        // Frame-rate constraint
        std::this_thread::sleep_for(frame_time);
    }

    return 0;
}
```

### What this demonstrates
- **Rigid body dynamics:** simplified to a single body with position and velocity
- **Gravity:** constant acceleration downward
- **Collision response:** bounce on the ground with restitution
- **Frame-rate constraints:** uses a target of **60 FPS**
- **Stable simulation:** uses a **fixed timestep** (`1/60s`) rather than variable integration steps

